stamp this  into an indexing pcr plate after this  indexing pcr plate is thermocycled  it then is transferred using the biomec  i7  into a another echo  low dead volume plate and we use the  echo  to pull an equal volume of each sample  we pull 500 nano liters of each sample  into a new destination plate and then  combine that into a single tube  where we do a single bead based cleanup  so after the speed based cleanup  uh the samples are quantified in qc'd  and go on to the sequencer  and so just want to note that in this  process we're actually only using  two tips total for this procedure the  first tip is used to transfer rna  for the 96 well plate into the low dead  volume plate  and the second tip is used to combine  the amplicons  dilute them and spot them into the  indexing pcr and the same box of tips  is then used to transfer the amplified  barcoded samples  into the echo ldd plate for transfer  and minimizing this tip uses really  helped with our our throughput as well  and we haven't had any issues with with  tip supplies  so in this next slide i just wanted to  briefly go over how the echo acoustic  liquid handler works because a lot of  people may not be familiar with it it's  a  essentially a random access liquid  handler that  transfers liquids using sound and so the  way this works is you have a  source plate uh that's an acoustic  qualified plate  and there are a couple different formats  in 3d for well format  and also a six wool reservoir for the  echo 525  that we're using there are also higher  and systems to transfer smaller volumes  they can use 15 36 well source plates  but for us the echo 5g5 works very well  it's kind of  it's a nice volume regime so this source  weight will go into the echo  and then next you put on your  destination plate  in our case we've been using these  384-wheel pcr plates this is put on the  um this little uh platform the platform  will grab  and grip the plate and then it'll flip  and rotate 180 degrees so that this  plate now faces  down the source plate will retract into  the instrument and in the instrument the  source plates down on the bottom and the  destination plate  that's upside down facing it are in  really close proximity  and below the source plate is a  transducer that will send pings of sound  energy  uh into the source well and this causes  really tiny 25 nanometer droplets to  get ejected from the meniscus straight  up and because the source plate and  destination plates are so close  facing each other uh there's very little  chance of cross-contamination happening  because a 25 nanometer droplet  essentially flies  straight up exits the source well and  enters the destination well  and so the system is really quick it can  transfer several hundred droplets  per second so copying a three or four  well plate  um and stamping out one mic leader only  takes a few minutes and again doesn't  use  any tips we've had some questions about  how we  um perform our reverse transcription  incubation in the echo load dead volume  plate and so  optimally you would have a flat block  thermocycler but there aren't many of  those  around typically in labs and so what we  did was we used a  bi-rad c-1000 384 wall thermocycler that  we had  and we essentially modified it by buying  some some things off of amazon  essentially because it was the fastest  way to get supplies  and so first thing we did was we took a  thin thermal pad  and so this is a relatively soft  conductive  thermally conductive pad that we cut to  size to put on  top of the 3d four wall thermocycler and  then on top of that we put a thin  aluminum shim stock on top to give it a  nice flat surface  and this is how we modified our c1000  thermocycler and then we put an echo ldv  plate on top that's a little hard to see  in the right hand side but it's um a  clear plate that's now sitting on top of  the shim  that's sitting on top of the thermal pad  so thermal pad will help with  with heat transfer and the shim provides  a nice flat surface  for the echo ldb plate to uh to sit on  top of  and we compared both a we compared both  running rt reactions in pcr plates as  well as the echo ldv plates and  we didn't see any changes in performance  and again the benefit here  is using one less piece of labware and  less tips that are required to do your  downstream transfer  and so in the uh  and in this next slide um uh what i'm  showing you on the left hand side is the  results i showed earlier from our tail  400 base method and on the right hand  side are those same samples that have  been run using our tail 275 base method  and so if you recall with the tail four  into base method we only had a  72 success rate but with the tailed um  275 method uh we actually had a 90  percent success rate again this is  dictated by a number of samples that  have greater than 92 percent  uh coverage of the star scope huge  genome and when we actually look at  samples  below ctf 30 our success rate is  actually 97.3  with this test set and down the bottom  is a little chart where we kind of broke  down  um the the samples according to ct bins  and you can see at the lower cts you  were getting very high  recovery of samples  and it's really once you get to cts  above 30  that the success rate starts to uh to  drop a bit  but we've been very happy with with this  protocol  and then using it to run a lot more  samples and so with the the current  status is  uh we're currently running 768 samples  um twice a week so our batches are two  three four well plates of samples at a  time  and we've been seeing above a 95 uh  success rate  uh again uh determined by more than 92  coverage  and so um our biggest limitation right  now is actually not in  our liquid handling but it's more in in  staff time so the staff  have to perform other duties as well and  so  they can only handle so many runs you  know per week  because of their their other duties but  the liquid handlers  buy our calculations if you had enough  staff you could easily process  eight three hundred eighty four plates  of samples uh per day  um we have pretty rapid turnaround time  with this method the um  384 768 samples can be  prepped and ready for sequencing in  about eight hours so it's about one  shift or one workday and again  really importantly we have reduced tip  usage we started off with three tips per  sample but realized we could actually  get that down to two tips per sample  and the method is a fairly low cost that  uses off-the-shelf reagents  and because we're working in 3d pro well  format uh we're working with 10  microliter reactions and so we have one  rt reaction  and three pcr reactions to the two  multiplex reactions  and one indexing pcr reaction um and  again  because we have the the echo it makes  pooling samples really easy  which means that we can pool or amplify  samples before cleanup  and do a single bead-based cleanup on a  combination of all 384 samples and so  a lot of this has been really enabled by  combining  the the unique aspects of our our  various  beckman liquid handlers a big one is the  echo 5g5 that allows  us to pull samples really quickly it  also allows us to transfer small volumes  with good accuracy as well as minimizing  contamination  and then uh we have been able to use our  biomec i7  and fx's to handle our 96 and 384 well  transfers  um sometimes i get the question that if  i was going to start over  um how would i set this up and if that  was the case i would probably just start  off with the  echo 525 and then a single biomic i7  with a dual arm that can be configured  with both a 3d pro wheel head and a 96  wall head because that would handle  all of our kind of liquid handling needs  uh so instead of having three  instruments we could actually make do  with  uh with two instruments all right so  um you know this has been been great and  so  um i've been thinking about some other  ways of increasing throughput mainly how  do you deal with  um maybe the uh um being shorthanded in  terms of staff time and how to really  leverage the  amount of capacity on the the automation  platforms and so one piece of automation  that i have  in another lab that's not not the cat is  a lapse a beckman access system and so  this has an echo 525 liquid handler  attached to it  as well as an access robotic arm that  can  feed many plates up to 80 or 100 plates  depending on your configuration  stacked in hotels the system also has an  automated  sealer and peeler and a plate centrifuge  as well as  a bulk dispenser that can be used to  transfer small volumes of bulk liquid  and so one thing that i envision  happening is if we want to really  increase throughput  while dealing with staff limitations is  to use a platform  uh like the beckman access system uh  they can handle  uh preparing your master mixes and  dispensing these into  many 380 fold plates these could be your  arctic  pcr amplicons one or two or your  indexing pcr  or your rt plate and then you can have  samples  in either 384 well echo  plates or on your to base liquid  handling to transfer them  in uh but this system could on the fly  create  your uh your mixes for you um and so  again  all of our amplifications are using a  standard pcr mix and  we just have a different type of primer  mix in there and so those could be local  located in acoustic source wells and  source plates um that are automatically  fed into the liquid handler  to create whatever plates you need kind  of on demand and also be able to handle  you know many many plates at a time  instead of the one or two three  different wool plates that we're running  in our current batches and so this has  the ability to really really increase  throughput  um to that next scale so we're  interested in playing around with  with ideas like this and so um with that  i'd like to acknowledge um a lot of the  zap that that did the work  um this was done um in the ucsf center  for advanced technology um and delta  martinez and tyler miyazaki  uh were really instrumental in in doing  the work and getting this done  i'd like to thank joe darici in his lab  they provided the initial 384 samples  that we compared as well as the data  using the standard arctic method  um amy kissler from cdc biohub for uh  great conversations  i'd like to really thank the idc team  from the chan zuckerberg  initiative that developed a um  essentially a cloud-based method that's  uh free for anybody to use to do sars  kobe 2  um genome analysis and so this is how  we've been uh calculating our our  coverage and they've been great  in terms of uploading our protocol our  specific protocol  um into their uh analysis platform and  lastly i'd also like to thank all the  members of the arctic network but you  know the work that we're doing here  uh with this method is really based on  all the groundwork uh that they've been  doing  again not just for star skill v2 but  even before stars kobe 2  uh with things like zika and dengue and  so they provided a really great  framework  uh to do a lot of this this  high-throughput sequencing  uh that most people around the world are  now using for for source code v2  uh and with that um thank you for  listening i'm happy to take any  questions  thank you eric for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you would like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  okay let's get started our first  question is  you mentioned improved throughput using  this method  in what way did echo help you with  improving your throughput  yeah that's a great question so um i'd  say there are a couple areas where  the uh echo acoustic liquid standard  really helped with uh  with throughput um you know first of all  again i mentioned i think a couple times  that it really  decreases tiff usage which can uh you  know be a limiting factor right now for  um i know a lot of groups are running  into chip shortages which is preventing  work  and without tips you've got no  throughput at all  and so by leveraging the the echo we've  actually been able to  cut down our tip usage per sample to  just two tips  for each and by decreasing the amount of  tips that we need  we haven't run into any supply  constraints the second area that it  really helps with  throughput is i'd say in the the pooling  steps so  at some point we need to pull all 3d  four ball samples together  um and so that can either use up a lot  of tips as well as take up a lot of time  if you're using the traditional to face  liquid handler but with the the echo  that pulling only takes about two  minutes  uh and so this has really helped as well  and  we also feel like the echo by the nature  of how it does the  the liquid transfers where the source  and destination plates  are really close together you really  minimize the  chance of contamination and often if you  have uh you know contamination your  controls uh what we and most other  groups will do  is have to repeat uh the run and so by  minimizing contamination you minimize  the number of reruns that you have to do  which also greatly increases throughput  the last thing we haven't done  yet that we're looking to do is how to  decrease our volumes  our current reaction volumes are  currently 10 microliters and that's kind  of a nice place to work with  with tip-based liquid handlers but  against reducing the echo we think  there's a possibility of decreasing that  by several fold  and that's probably next on our list to  to help increase  our our throughput  thank you eric our next question  how much volume can you hold in a  destination plate upside down when  pooling  and what type of plate are you using as  the destination plate  yeah so um for um the work that we do  um we're using buy rad 96 or 3d for well  plates for our our destination plates  and really with the echo you can use  pretty much any type of sbs format plate  that you um you want to work with  as long as i think they're below like 14  millimeters in in height that's the  the only limitation but you know pretty  much any  fully started pcr flight is is going to  work and we go up to 15 microliters  in volume for the destination plate uh  just so we don't fill it up too much  and um we just want to make sure that  you don't fill it up so much that you  get  you know dripping at the end so with 15  microliters uh  is a very safe volume that we haven't  seen any you know drips or anything like  that happening with the system  thank you eric our next question  after the pandemic how do you see using  this workflow to sequence other  pathogens  yeah so um yeah there's something we've  been thinking about  uh as well so the cat's done a lot of  kind of metagenomic sequencing in the  past we'll try to look for for other  pathogens and  um star scale ob2 was our first  experience  uh with this arctic network protocol and  method  and something we realize is that what's  great about this method is that it's  really a framework you know you can use  essentially the same procedures for any  pathogen you just design  a new set of multiplex primers your  reaction mixes are all identical  um you know for all the different steps  right whether it's an rt reaction  a multiplex vcr reaction or an indexing  pcr reaction so the only difference is  your samples  and then your primer sets and so once  you've designed your primer sets  within the same run again leveraging the  kind of  random access nature of the the echo you  could use the echo  to process many different types of  samples on a single plate because you  use the echo  to spot out your uh your multiplex pcr  mix  um into the the wells that you need for  your particular pathogen  and again for those who haven't worked  with an echo or  a uh a liquid handler with like a spande  multi-channel head that can do cherry  picking  what you do is you just feed a  spreadsheet that just instructs the  system what type of sample  this and what well and that'll tell the  system which primer set to deposit into  that well  and so we think that could be you know  one way of setting this up  especially if you have a higher  throughput you know access system where  you can load multiple plates  in hotels at once place contain your  your samples your destination rt plates  or pcr plates  as well as source plates containing your  indexing primers and your multiplex pcr  primers for different pathogens  all right thank you eric our next  question  you mentioned you only use two tips how  many in total  if you also count the extraction part  yeah that's a good question so that's  going to depend on your method of of  extraction  and so again this protocol that i'm  describing using two tips  is only starting from uh purified rna  so we haven't tried doing any type of uh  direct amplification from  say like saliva or heat inactivated  extracts  um but i've seen different protocols for  rna extraction  ranging from as many as you know 14 15  tips  uh all the way down to two tips uh  depending on the uh the protocol that  you were you're using  but you know what we think would be a  workflow would be most groups would do  dpcr testing that would require rna  traction anyways and you have rna left  over  and after you get your positive hits  determined by qpcr you can go back to  those source plates and pull out the  purified rna  and then go into your your sequencing  workflow  all right thank you eric our next  question can you talk a little bit  of the liquid handler software how was  your experience programming the liquid  handlers  yeah so um you know one thing we really  like about the uh the beckman systems  is the kind of ease of use so the the  cad is actually an  open access technology facility we  typically don't do  a sample prep um but you know i think  the pandemic kind of  changed the roles we all play and so we  kind of jumped in to do  uh stars kobe 2 sequencing because of  the need but  traditionally in the cat all the  instruments that we have including  our liquid handlers are used by anybody  on campus and we provide training  and so for the tip-based liquid handlers  on the fx and the i-series  we typically just do a one-hour training  with users who want to learn to use the  platform  and that's enough to get them going to  start writing their own protocols so  it's relatively  straightforward because of the kind of  graphical user interface you don't have  to  know much or anything about scripting  for instance or programming  on the echoes even easier it's a little  less dangerous because you don't have to  worry about tip crash you just right  because you're not working with with  tips and then our training for that  ranges from 30 minutes to one hour  uh to get people started so it's um  pretty easy to  to get started on via the protocol uh  and  and start to program these instruments  which we we really like in the context  of a core facility with many users  all right thank you again eric next  question  what volume did you run your rt reaction  yeah so um all of our reaction volumes  were 10 microliters so these included  the  um the rt reaction um that was run in  the echo low dead volume plates  so we started out with two microliters  of the uh um  of the uh 5x uh this lunascript master  mix that we  we use and then we add eight microliters  of rna on top of that so that was used  in that first  biomec fx 96 well liquid handler to do  that that transfer  and then the subsequent multiplex pcrs  were 10 microliters as well as the  indexing pcr  and um again i think there's probably  definitely a lot of room to further  decrease those volumes if you want to  want to drop costs just because of the  ease of  lower volume transfers with the echo and  so that's something we're uh we're  playing around with as well  great thank you again eric we have time  for one more question  do you anticipate decrease of your  sequencing throughput as the pandemic  winds down in north america  yeah that would be the the hope right  that we can all give back to  uh to our normal lives but uh i would  say that even if uh you know case rates  go down  by a lot you know right now i don't even  know if in this country we're sequencing  five percent of of all the positives  so you know ideally you could you would  sequence as many of the positive cases  um  as you can so i'd say that you know  even if you were to decrease number of  positive cases by  uh another 20 fold we might be able to  sequence 100  of uh of all the samples so i'd say that  i don't think anytime  soon we'll be kind of decreasing the  need for for sequencing again i think  it's going to be important to also  continue sequencing um you know positive  cases  uh especially among vaccinated  individuals and to be honest you know we  we totally expect to see positive cases  among vaccinated  individuals just because you know the  the vaccines aren't 100  uh you know perfect so what we want to  do is just make sure that there isn't  some maybe nasty variant that  can escape the vaccine again there's no  uh variant that escapes the vaccine so  everyone should still go out and get  vaccinated but  i think the surveillance is going to be  important just to um detect  if if that does happen so i'd say that  you know we're going to continue  sequencing for  the time going forward and there's also  a need for doing a lot of sequencing  um outside of of north america as well  just around the world so you know  hopefully some of these methods  uh and platforms can become a use for  for people  outside of north america too  all right thanks again eric do you have  any final comments for our audience  before we go  uh no i just wanted to um you know thank  the audience for uh  tuning in today and listening and then  also just wanted to again  acknowledge um the people who worked on  on this project and also  again thank the uh the groups around uh  working around the at the arctic network  so there's a group of individuals who've  been working for for several years and  other pathogens and really shifted focus  on starch cov2  and have really built a lot of great  protocols and resources then and and  share them widely  um just to make it accessible to uh to  all groups i yeah i definitely want to  thank them for for all their hard work  as well  well thank you again dr eric ciao for  your time today and for your important  research  we would also like to thank labyrinth  and our sponsor beckmann culture life  sciences  for underwriting today's educational  webcast before we go  i'd like to thank the audience for  joining us today and for their  interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

